prs.body:
Closes #195643

## What Problem This Solves

Fixes WhatsApp rejecting a linked administrator's `automations` requests as “this turn was not started by the owner.”

## User Impact

WhatsApp senders linked to a profile with current `operator.admin` authority can manage automations through the same authority path as other verified channels. No configuration or database migration is required.

## Why This Change Was Made

WhatsApp already resolves the authenticated sender's canonical phone from the socket session, but its ingress descriptor classifies that primary as an asserted phone alias. Shared ingress only resolves linked-profile authority from a verified stable primary, so the lookup introduced by #153508 is skipped.

Use the stable primary default with verified authentication, following the existing Telegram ingress pattern. These are separate properties: the primary kind identifies the canonical channel principal, while authentication records the transport proof. Verified authentication alone grants no administrator role; the existing account-scoped profile link and current role still decide authority. Keep phone normalization, PII redaction and the shared live-authority checks. This repairs the producer without weakening the owner guard or changing automation ownership.

## Evidence

- On base `df13b6b8994cefe16f311f29f0301bba511ec617`, both real-normalizer/SDK regressions fail with `kind: phone` and `subjectAuthentication: asserted`; both pass with the fix.
- WhatsApp regression and access tests: **16 passed**. Shared `operator-authority.test.ts`: **47 passed**. Format, targeted lint, WhatsApp package and regression-test type checks, assertion-safety and test-line checks passed. The initial CI failure was the missing allocation for the new 36-line regression; commit `40704b5` adds the documented dedicated allocation without changing the shared scalar. CI is green on that head.
- Independent **autoreview**, candidate against `df13b6b8994cefe16f311f29f0301bba511ec617`, coverage **P0–P2**: `scoped-clean`, no findings.

### Automation effects and retained-authority rejection

An in-process fixture now follows the actual WhatsApp normalizer and producer through host ingress/context binding, SQLite-backed profile links/current roles, run admission, native `automations` tool guards, Gateway dispatch/`cron.remove`, real `CronService`, and the SQLite cron row. It invokes the tool, not just owner/requester resolution. All fixtures are disabled with no delivery; no model or production data is used.

| WhatsApp principal | Owner at admission | Result at automation effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Linked administrator, DM | true | Native removal succeeds; persisted fixture row removed |
| Linked administrator, group | true | Native removal succeeds; persisted fixture row removed |
| Same phone, different account | false | Owner gate rejects; CronService removal never called; row unchanged |
| Unlinked sender | false | Owner gate rejects; CronService removal never called; row unchanged |
| Linked profile demoted before admission | false | Owner gate rejects; CronService removal never called; row unchanged |
| Link removed during awaited request | true | Retained authority rejected; row unchanged |
| Link reassigned to another admin during awaited request | true | Retained authority rejected; row unchanged |
| Profile demoted during awaited request | true | Retained authority rejected; row unchanged |

The last three cases pause at the real service removal entry, after the Gateway has prepared its mutation guard. They revoke the actual SQLite-backed link/role, then resume the unchanged service method with that guard. Each rejects with `Channel operator authority changed; send a new request.` The raw stored cron rows compare equal before/after. No mocked authorization, result, or SQL mutation is supplied.

Restoring the base WhatsApp producer makes the same allowed DM removal fail with `{gated:true}` and the persisted row retained. Restoring the committed fix makes that native removal succeed again. The fixture also asserts that its row exists before calling the tool.

The transport socket/group metadata and Gateway context wiring are synthetic test fixtures. The authority, tool, handler and mutation implementations are production code. This is reproducible in-process Gateway proof, **not a live WhatsApp network test or a production deployment**. Account isolation and revocation are exercised at the final cron-write boundary.

<details>
<summary>Terminal evidence: eight final-effect cases</summary>

```jsonl
{"case":"allowed","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":true,"effect":"committed; row removed","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":false,"removeCalls":1,"result":{"ok":true,"removed":true}}
{"case":"allowed-group","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":true,"effect":"committed; row removed","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":false,"removeCalls":1,"result":{"ok":true,"removed":true}}
{"case":"wrong-account","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":false,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":false,"removeCalls":0,"denial":[{"type":"text","text":"automations needs the owner's authority, and this turn was not started by the owner. Do not retry automations."}]}
{"case":"unlinked","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":false,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":false,"removeCalls":0,"denial":[{"type":"text","text":"automations needs the owner's authority, and this turn was not started by the owner. Do not retry automations."}]}
{"case":"demoted","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":false,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":false,"removeCalls":0,"denial":[{"type":"text","text":"automations needs the owner's authority, and this turn was not started by the owner. Do not retry automations."}]}
{"case":"retained-unlink","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":true,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":true,"removeCalls":1,"error":"Error: Channel operator authority changed; send a new request."}
{"case":"retained-reassign","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":true,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":true,"removeCalls":1,"error":"Error: Channel operator authority changed; send a new request."}
{"case":"retained-demote","admitted":true,"senderIsOwner":true,"effect":"denied; row retained","revokedAfterGatewayAwait":true,"removeCalls":1,"error":"Error: Channel operator authority changed; send a new request."}
```

</details>

<details>
<summary>Reproducible native fixture</summary>

At head `40704b549334f5e43340bff5231e30ae870647a8`, save this as `/tmp/whatsapp-authority-effect.mts` and run from the checkout:

```sh
node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs /tmp/whatsapp-authority-effect.mts
```

`PROOF_CASES=allowed` selects the red/green effect case. Replacing only `extensions/whatsapp/src/inbound-policy.ts` with its base version reproduces the rejected removal; restore the committed producer before running the green case. Fixture profiles, phone numbers and group identifiers below are synthetic. Test state is isolated and cleaned up by `withOpenClawTestState`.

```ts
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import path from 'node:path';
import { pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';

// Synthetic transport and fixture entities; real authorization, tools, Gateway handlers and SQLite mutation.
// Run from the OpenClaw checkout. No model, live network, or production state is used.
const load = (p: string) => import(pathToFileURL(path.resolve(process.cwd(), p)).href);
const { withOpenClawTestState } = await load('src/test-utils/openclaw-test-state.ts');
const { createHostChannelIngressRuntime } = await load('src/channels/message-access/runtime.ts');
const { createHostChannelInboundEventContextBuilder } = await load('src/channels/inbound-event/host-context-builder.ts');
const { buildChannelInboundEventContext } = await load('src/channels/inbound-event/context.ts');
const { resolveCommandAuthorization } = await load('src/auto-reply/command-auth.ts');
const { captureCommandOwnerAssertion } = await load('src/auto-reply/command-owner-authority.ts');
const { prepareInternalGetReplyOptions } = await load('src/auto-reply/reply/get-reply.types.ts');
const { prepareChannelRunAdmission } = await load('src/auto-reply/reply/channel-run-admission.ts');
const { readAdmittedRunOperatorAuthority, resolveAdmittedRunActiveAssertion } = await load('src/agents/admitted-run-context.ts');
