## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: the secret egress proxy returns `502 Bad Gateway` during CONNECT certificate preparation for allowed hostnames longer than the certificate CommonName limit.

## User Impact

Valid long hostnames can establish verified proxy TLS without being moved to `bypassHosts`. The existing exact-host allowlist, process authentication, trust root, certificate lifetime, and revocation behavior are unchanged.

## Why This Change Was Made

The certificate owner now uses a short descriptive CommonName while retaining the complete canonical hostname in subjectAltName. SAN remains the TLS identity; the CommonName field no longer imposes an unrelated 64-character limit on valid DNS names. No new configuration, dependency, permission, trust-store installation, cache policy, or retained state is introduced.

## Evidence

- Actual authenticated HTTP CONNECT and native Node TLS through the existing Gateway secret-egress runtime/server and canonical process-registration owner, using fresh synthetic state and a task-created CA. An allowed 86-character hostname returned 502 before the change, with the real `openssl req` child reporting the CommonName limit; afterward CONNECT returned 200 and default TLS verification reported `authorized: true` for the exact full hostname.
- Short-host verified TLS remained successful. Missing, bad, and revoked proxy credentials still returned 407; an unlisted host returned 403; a wrong client hostname failed with `ERR_TLS_CERT_ALTNAME_INVALID`. A repeated long-host connection reused its valid leaf.
- No decrypted HTTP request was sent inside the tunnel, and no upstream DNS/network request occurred. Every client socket and OpenSSL child settled; runtime shutdown cleared the registry and removed the task CA directory. No operator state or system trust store was changed.
- The real-OpenSSL/X509 hostname regression fails against the original owner. All 75 focused certificate, proxy, registration-lifecycle, and background-exec lifecycle tests passed after the repair. Scoped type-aware lint, formatting, whitespace, both source line-limit ratchets, and executable-runtime builds passed.

The protocol proof used current-main dependencies, Node 24.21.0, and the system `openssl` command (LibreSSL 3.3.6) on the development host, with certificate/proxy/process owners matching this PR. It is not a full replay of the PR's older base, a full Gateway/agent-exec launch, an upstream upload or stored-secret substitution, an original Linux deployment test, or a universal custom TLS-client compatibility claim. Full static and matrix integration remains with hosted CI.

