## Storage contract and maintainer decision

The [storage review checkpoint](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/878114675c81e88c78e29d73a76cc0f530a80b8f/docs/reference/database-schemas/storage-changes.md#review-checkpoint-for-material-changes) applies because the retained content and admission failure behavior change, despite an unchanged numeric schema. **Maintainer acceptance is pending.** The evidence below is submitted for that decision; contributor verification does not supply approval.

Please record whether these two behaviors are accepted:

1. Retain JSON-quoted unfinished assistant output inside the visible, durable timeout report, and replay that report into later model context.
2. Reject a successor that observes a failed timeout-report write before dispatch, with the error/cleanup/recovery limits below.

| Contract topic | Proposed behavior and boundary |
| --- | --- |
| Owner and canonical record | The existing terminal lifecycle owner supplies authority; the existing report transaction commits one canonical custom record containing outcome and captured output. UI/model history are projections. |
| Alternatives considered | Keeping text in memory loses it after cleanup/restart. Injecting a separate assistant row creates a partial/report inter-write window and encounters existing failed-assistant replay filtering. Dispatching while the report is pending permits stale next-turn context. |
| Retention and deletion | The report follows existing transcript/session retention and compaction. There is no independent store or retention policy. Persisted report text can outlive the in-memory buffer. |
| Ordering and concurrency | Matching agent/session admission waits outside the session writer before and after acquiring work admission. Existing authority validation and deduplication remain in force. Already-dispatched queued successors are outside this proof. |
| Failed write | The test injects a rejected report operation before the underlying report write. The observed waiting RPC returns `INVALID_REQUEST`; no successor provider request starts. This is not a physical disk-failure or interrupted-commit experiment. |
| Cleanup and recovery | The old run's registration is removed on settlement; the rejected successor releases its reservation/lease. Source inspection shows this is a wait on the observed run, not a permanent session lock. A later independent request uses normal admission after cleanup. No automatic report retry, durable recovery queue, restored partial buffer, or successful post-failure retry is claimed or added here. |
| Crash window | A crash before the report commits can lose the in-memory output/outcome. Session status and transcript are not made one atomic transaction. Existing assistant rows are not rewritten. |
| Storage and latency | One report per timed-out run; retained content grows with the JSON-quoted buffer plus a fixed label. Waiting successors incur the observed report-write latency. There is no production storage/latency benchmark or new per-report truncation policy in this PR. |
| Upgrade and rollback | Existing agent DBs use the established schema migration owner. A schema-19 released reader refuses schema 23. Recovery to that release restores the pre-upgrade backup and excludes post-backup writes; same-schema main-parent readers can read/write the reports. No installed-updater or shared-state upgrade claim. |

